├── async web framework with WebSocket support
│   ├── Candidates : flask, quart
│   ├── Used APIs  : Flask, SocketIO, render_template, emit, close_room, disconnect, join_room, leave_room, request, session
│   ├── Pitfalls   : Quart is async-native; migrating requires converting sync route handlers to async def; Flask-SocketIO uses 
│   │   Socket.IO protocol (fallbacks, rooms, auto-reconnect); Quart uses raw WebSockets — protocol change affects clients; gevent
│   │   monkey-patching must be removed; Quart runs on asyncio/uvloop; Background thread pattern (Thread + time.sleep) must become
│   │   async task with asyncio.sleep; session and request context work differently in async context
│   ├── Constraints: requires_connector
│   └── Quart is an async reimplementation of the Flask API with native WebSocket support. It provides drop-in compatibility for 
│       Flask routes, templates, sessions, and request context. The code uses Flask-SocketIO for real-time features; Quart has 
│       built-in WebSocket support that can replace Flask-SocketIO entirely, though the API differs (native websocket vs SocketIO 
│       protocol).
└── greenlet-based concurrency library for monkey-patching stdlib
    ├── Candidates : gevent, eventlet
    ├── Used APIs  : monkey.patch_all
    ├── Pitfalls   : Eventlet has different greenlet implementation; some edge cases in C extensions may behave differently; 
    │   monkey.patch_all() arguments differ slightly (e.g., eventlet doesn't patch 'thread' module by default in newer versions); 
    │   Eventlet is less actively maintained than gevent; consider long-term viability; If migrating to Quart (asyncio), both 
    │   gevent and eventlet must be removed entirely — they are incompatible with asyncio event loop
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── Eventlet is the closest functional alternative to gevent — both provide greenlet-based cooperative multitasking and 
        monkey-patch the standard library (socket, time, threading, etc.) to make blocking calls cooperative. The code only uses 
        monkey.patch_all() to enable async I/O for Flask-SocketIO. Eventlet is also a supported async_mode for Flask-SocketIO.

